1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
You buy a new game. You press Start, grip the
controller,

2
00:00:04,089 --> 00:00:06,320
and three minutes later you're still watching
a cutscene.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,680
Your hands are ready, but there's nothing to
do.

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:11,920
By now my patience is moving faster than the
character.

5
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:15,460
Masahiro Sakurai, creator of Smash Bros. and
Kirby, offers advice rooted in

6
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:22,460
arcade development: a game should show why
it's fun within the first three minutes.

7
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:27,040
Players don't judge fun by listening to
explanations. They try it and decide.

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,980
Make them wait too long, and they leave before
they've even judged.

9
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:36,220
Take Super Mario Odyssey. After a short
opening, Mario lands in the Cap Kingdom,

10
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:38,820
and from there the running and jumping are in
the player's hands.

11
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:44,260
Today, following Odyssey, let's look at how to
design a game's first three minutes.

12
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:50,640
This isn't only about what happens inside the
game. Think of a trailer on an online store.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,180
Beautiful graphics and story go on for a
while, and what you actually do only appears

14
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:01,020
near the end. Sakurai says that's why he got
into the habit of skipping the beginning.

15
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:05,920
What the viewer wants to know comes down to
one thing: so, what do you do in this game?

16
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:11,660
Odyssey answers that in seconds. Throw your
cap and coins pop and crates break;

17
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,940
throw it at an enemy and you become that
enemy.

18
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,800
A scene where you can tell what to do after a
few

19
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,520
seconds with the sound off is the best first
hello.

20
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:24,140
The visuals can come after. Answer the
curiosity first.

21
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,080
That doesn't mean cutting the story. Some
genres,

22
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:30,420
like RPGs, genuinely need an introduction.

23
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:32,500
There are ways to handle that. One is to put a
moment

24
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:35,760
where things are already happening at the very
start.

25
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,580
Odyssey opens on an airship in the middle of a
fight. It doesn't spend long

26
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:42,920
explaining why Bowser wants a wedding. That
part is a short cutscene,

27
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:48,580
not gameplay, but it drops you straight into
the action all the same.

28
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:54,460
Sakurai goes a step further: bring a later
moment forward and let people play it,

29
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,160
or let them control someone who fought before
the hero.

30
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:02,460
It's fine if the player doesn't know what's
going on yet.

31
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:06,260
Once it's in their hands, the urge to find out
actually carries the story forward.

32
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,230
Keep the story, but pull the controls forward.

33
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:12,860
The order of events and the order of play
don't have to match.

34
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,380
Why put play so far forward?

35
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:20,360
Films and animation can show great visuals and
stories too. But a character moving

36
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:25,840
when you press a button, and a world reacting
to you, is something only games can offer.

37
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:28,400
In the Cascade Kingdom, throw your cap at a
dinosaur and now you are the dinosaur,

38
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:35,220
smashing through whatever blocked the way.
That's a feel you can't get just by watching.

39
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:39,640
The first input is the game's promise.

40
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:42,740
That first feel of movement tells players what
they'll be doing from here on.

41
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:48,840
So in the first few minutes, let players taste
the part no other medium can replace.

42
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:53,520
Of course, the answer differs by game. In a
genre whose controls are already familiar,

43
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:57,460
drawing people in with staging or story first
can work well.

44
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,620
What matters is not an intro that happened to
get long,

45
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,680
but deciding on purpose what to show first.

46
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,740
The same goes for tutorials.

47
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:08,020
The Cap Kingdom doesn't set aside practice for
cap throws and captures;

48
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:12,880
it lets you use them right on the main path.
We'll cover that separately another time.

49
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,560
Here's a fun detail: when you start a new
game, the Super Mario Odyssey title

50
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:22,100
sequence only appears after you beat the first
boss. Play comes before the title.

51
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:26,820
Try timing your own game's first three
minutes. After pressing Start,

52
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,680
when does the player first move, and does that
movement tell them why the game is fun?

53
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,460
Don't make them wait. Let them play first.
